---
// One icon component for every mark on the site. Two sources, one hand:
//
// - Custom marks (the three hero flow tiles: catalogue, agent, customer) are
//   drawn on a 24x24 canvas at a 1.7 stroke, the weight the site has always
//   used. The agent wears the same four-point spark as its chat avatar
//   (AgentAvatar.astro) so the tile and the speaking avatar read as one
//   character; it smiles because a neutral robot face read as flat and sad
//   (user decision, 2026-08-29).
// - Phosphor marks (https://phosphoricons.com, MIT) are copied in as static
//   duotone path data on Phosphor's 256x256 canvas. Duotone lays a 20% fill
//   under the 16-unit stroke, which gives every tile a second tone from
//   currentColor alone - the warmth the flat outline set lacked. No package,
//   no runtime, no request: the paths ship inline like every other mark here.
//
// Two ways to render:
//   <Icon name="plug" tile="peach" size={40} />  - a tinted rounded tile
//   <Icon name="check-circle" />                 - inline, 1em, beside text
//
// A tile's svg fills the tile and carries its inset in the viewBox, never a
// smaller centred box: a centred box is positioned and rounded on its own and
// snapped off-centre at fractional layout positions (user-reported,
// 2026-08-23). The inset sets the glyph to about 61% of the tile on both
// canvases: 24-unit art uses -7.7647 (24 * 28/17), and 256-unit Phosphor art,
// whose ink spans ~87% of its canvas, uses -57.5 (256 / 0.69) so both sets
// land at the same optical size.
//
// Every icon is decorative: the adjacent label names the step, so the glyph
// stays out of the accessibility tree.

